from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "4. Matthew 10:28 and Philippians 3:19"),
("p", "Claim: the two verses most often brought forward for the destruction of the lost state a power and an outcome, and neither states the manner or the length of that outcome. This chapter reads them in their own settings, concedes what the other side rightly takes from them, and says what the volume does and does not build on them."),
("sub", "4.1 The setting of Matthew 10"),
("p", "The Lord’s words in Matthew 10:28 are spoken in a commission. He has called the Twelve and sent them out with an instruction that fixes their ground: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 10:5, 6). Their message is “The kingdom of heaven is at hand” (Matthew 10:7), and their works are to “Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils” (Matthew 10:8). The chapter then turns to what will befall them. They are sent “as sheep in the midst of wolves” (Matthew 10:16), they will be delivered up to councils and scourged in synagogues, brought before governors and kings, and hated of all men for the Lord’s name’s sake. They are told, “Ye shall not have gone over the cities of Israel, till the Son of man be come” (Matthew 10:23)."),
("p", "Everything in the chapter so far is addressed to Israel’s messengers in Israel’s cities, in the Kingdom ministry, and the volume reads the verse that follows in that setting. It is a witness to what the Lord says of the fate of the wicked. It is not a word to the Body of Christ and it is not applied to it. The chapter also speaks of the day of judgment in its own terms: of a city that will not receive the Twelve the Lord says, “It shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrha in the day of judgment, than for that city” (Matthew 10:15). A judgment with degrees is already in view before the verse on destroying soul and body is reached."),
("sub", "4.2 What Matthew 10:28 states"),
("p", "The verse reads, “And fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell” (Matthew 10:28). It is built on a contrast. The first party can kill the body and can do no more. The second party is able to destroy both soul and body in hell, and for that reason is to be feared. The persecutors named in the chapter are the first party. The second is God. The sentence is a ground for courage: the Twelve are not to be afraid of what men can do, because what God can do is greater, and He is the one to whom they answer."),
("tbl", "The two parties of Matthew 10:28 and the parallel in Luke 12.", [
    ("Matthew 10:28", T("Matt 10:28"), "The first party kills the body and is not able to kill the soul; the second is able to destroy both soul and body in hell."),
    ("Luke 12:4", T("Luke 12:4"), "The first party kills the body and after that has no more that they can do."),
    ("Luke 12:5", T("Luke 12:5"), "The one to be feared, after he hath killed, hath power to cast into hell."),
  ], [0.18, 0.52, 0.30]),
("p", "Three things are stated. The first is a power: the verse says “able”. It does not say that this power is exercised in a given case, or on whom. The second is that the object of the power is “both soul and body”, which is more than the persecutors can touch. The third is the place: “in hell”, the word the Gospels use for the valley of Hinnom and the judgment it stands for. What the verse does not state is what the destroying consists of, how long it lasts, and whether the one destroyed continues. Those are not in the sentence."),
("sub", "4.3 The parallel in Luke 12"),
("p", "Luke records the same teaching in another setting. The Lord is speaking “unto his disciples first of all” while an innumerable multitude is gathered, and He begins, “Beware ye of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy” (Luke 12:1). The same ground is given for courage: “there is nothing covered, that shall not be revealed” (Luke 12:2). Then comes the saying: “Be not afraid of them that kill the body, and after that have no more that they can do” (Luke 12:4), followed by “Fear him, which after he hath killed hath power to cast into hell; yea, I say unto you, Fear him” (Luke 12:5)."),
("p", "The two accounts give the same two parties and the same two fears, in different words. Where Matthew says that God is able to destroy soul and body in hell, Luke says that He, after He hath killed, hath power to cast into hell. The volume reads the second as shedding light on the first. In Luke the power is stated in two steps, a killing and then a casting, and the casting is into hell. In Matthew the destroying is “in hell”. The place is the same and the power is the same. The volume holds that this is shown by the context and not Stated: the Lord does not say in either place that the two sayings are one, and a reader who takes the Matthew verse alone is not wrong to ask what the destroying is. The parallel is the nearest answer the text gives, and it describes a casting, not a ceasing."),
("sub", "4.4 What the other side rightly takes from the verse"),
("p", "A writer who holds that the lost cease to exist reads the verse as the plainest statement of his case. God is able to destroy the soul. If the soul can be destroyed, it is not by nature beyond the reach of its Maker, and the ordinary idea that the soul cannot die is not found in this verse. The volume grants both points. Matthew 10:28 does not teach that the soul is indestructible, and nothing in this volume rests on the idea that it is. The verse also treats the soul and the body together as the object of the power, which is the opposite of the picture in which the body perishes and the soul goes on untouched."),
("p", "The writer goes a step further and reads “destroy” as the act of ending the person, and “able” as a statement of what God will do. The first step is possible. The second is a conclusion the verse does not draw. A power is not an outcome, and the Lord in the same chapter speaks of degrees in the day of judgment, which is a statement about the condition of those judged and not only about their removal. The volume therefore does not say that Matthew 10:28 proves a continuing experience. It says that the verse states a power and a place, that Luke 12:5 describes the exercise of the power as a casting into hell, and that neither verse says what the person is afterwards."),
("sub", "4.5 The word destruction in other places"),
("p", "The noun that stands at the root of the question is used in the New Testament in more than one way, and the uses bear on what a reader may take from it. Of the ointment poured on the Lord’s head the disciples said, “To what purpose is this waste?” (Matthew 26:8), and another account records, “Why was this waste of the ointment made?” (Mark 14:4). The ointment was not annihilated; it was spent without profit, and the word names that. Of Simon the sorcerer Peter said, “Thy money perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money” (Acts 8:20), where the perishing is the condemnation of a man who is standing there. The Lord says of the apostles He had kept, “none of them is lost, but the son of perdition” (John 17:12), and the loss named there is spoken of a living disciple. In the letter to the Romans the vessels of wrath are described as “fitted to destruction” and as having been endured “with much longsuffering” (Romans 9:22), and vessels that are endured are there to be endured."),
("p", "These uses do not decide the sense of the word at Matthew 10:28. They show that the noun does not always mean an extinction. In a verse where a person is lost, wasted, condemned or fitted for ruin, the word names what has happened to him, and the question whether he continues is not answered by the word. The question has to be answered by the passage."),
("sub", "4.6 Philippians 3:19"),
("p", "The second verse comes from a letter. Paul writes to the saints at Philippi and gives them a pattern: “Brethren, be followers together of me, and mark them which walk so as ye have us for an ensample” (Philippians 3:17). He then speaks of others: “many walk, of whom I have told you often, and now tell you even weeping, that they are the enemies of the cross of Christ: Whose end is destruction, whose God is their belly, and whose glory is in their shame, who mind earthly things” (Philippians 3:18, 19). The contrast follows at once: “For our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, the Lord Jesus Christ: Who shall change our vile body, that it may be fashioned like unto his glorious body” (Philippians 3:20, 21)."),
("p", "The verse names an end and calls it destruction. It describes those whose end it is in four phrases, each of them a description of a way of life, and it sets them against the saints, whose end is a changed body and a Saviour from heaven. The word translated “end” is the outcome of a course, and the verse states that the outcome of the course it describes is destruction. Paul had already used the same noun in the same letter. Of the saints’ adversaries he says that their courage is “an evident token of perdition, but to you of salvation, and that of God” (Philippians 1:28). In each place the word stands over against salvation, and in each place it names the opposite of being saved."),
("p", "The verse is read as written. It states that the enemies of the cross have an end and that the end is destruction. It does not describe the destruction. It does not say that the person ceases, and it does not say that the person continues. Nor is it a warning to the saints at Philippi; the letter speaks of them as those whose conversation is in heaven, and the verse describes others. The volume draws from it only what it states, that Paul sets the end of the enemies of the cross against the end of the saints and calls the first destruction."),
("sub", "4.7 What the two verses leave open"),
("p", "The reader who has followed the chapter will see what has been done. Two verses that carry much of the weight in the conditionalist case have been read in their settings and given their full force: the soul can be destroyed by God, and the end of the enemies of the cross is destruction. Neither has been made to say more than it says. The verse in Matthew states a power and a place and is paralleled by a verse that describes the exercise of the power as casting into hell. The verse in Philippians states an end and a name. Whether the destruction is a ceasing or a ruin that remains is not settled by either. It is settled, if it is settled, by the passages that go on to describe what follows the judgment, and the next chapters turn to them."),
("ctbl", "The two verses compared.", [
    ("", "Matthew 10:28", "Philippians 3:19"),
    ("Who speaks", "The Lord, to the Twelve", "Paul, to the saints at Philippi"),
    ("Of whom", "The One who is able to destroy; the persecutors are the other party", "The enemies of the cross of Christ"),
    ("What is stated", "A power: able to destroy soul and body in hell", "An end: whose end is destruction"),
    ("What is set against it", "The persecutors, who kill the body and can do no more", "The saints, whose conversation is in heaven"),
    ("What is not stated", "That the power is exercised; the manner; the length; whether the person continues", "The manner; the length; whether the person continues"),
    ("Setting", "The Twelve’s mission to Israel, in the Kingdom ministry", "A letter to saints, with the enemies of the cross described as others"),
  ], [0.2, 0.4, 0.4]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Matthew 10:28 is spoken to the Twelve on their mission to Israel; it is a witness to the fate of the wicked and is not applied to the Body.",
    "The verse states a power of God and a place, and treats the soul and the body as the object of the power; it does not teach that the soul is indestructible.",
    "Luke 12:5 describes the same power as a casting into hell after the killing, and the volume holds this shown by the context.",
    "The noun for destruction is used of waste, of a condemned man, of a lost disciple and of vessels endured, so the word alone does not decide.",
    "Philippians 3:19 states an end and calls it destruction, setting it against the saints’ end; it does not describe the manner or the length.",
    "Neither verse is made to prove a continuing experience, and neither is made to prove a ceasing.",
  ]),
]
